IMPORT prelude

-- Sale of Goods Act 1979 (Singapore) -- what the Act gives, and what it can make me pay, when I buy goods: whether the Act reaches my
-- purchase (ss 1(1), 2(1), 61(1) "goods", 62(4)), what price I must pay (ss 3(2), 8), goods that perish (ss 6, 7), what the seller
-- promises about title (s 12), the implied conditions of description, satisfactory quality, fitness for purpose and sample (ss 13, 14,
-- 15) with the slight-breach rule (s 15A), whether I can still reject or only claim damages (ss 11(3), 35), when the property and the
-- risk pass to me (ss 17, 18 Rules 1 to 5, 20), whether I get good title from a seller who did not own the goods (ss 21, 23, 24, 25),
-- the wrong quantity (s 30), examining the goods (s 34), returning rejected goods (s 36), what the seller can claim from me (ss 37,
-- 49, 50), my damages (ss 51, 53) and auctions (s 57).
--
-- SEAT: the buyer -- the individual who buys or agrees to buy goods (s 61(1) "buyer") and asks what the Act gives them and what it can
-- make them pay. Every decision is phrased as the buyer would ask it, on facts the buyer could state.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, version in force from 1 April 2022, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/SGA1979.txt. The cover says the revised edition "incorporates all amendments up to and including
-- 1 December 2021" and came into operation on 31 December 2021; the latest amendment annotated is Act 25 of 2021 wef 01/04/2022.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: capacity beyond necessaries (s 3(1), general law), formalities (s 4), future and contingent goods (s 5), valuation by a
-- third party (s 9), time stipulations (s 10), waiver of a condition (s 11(1)), the s 14(3)(b) credit-broker limb, usage (s 14(4)),
-- reservation of the right of disposal (s 19), undivided shares in bulk (ss 20A, 20B), the Factors Act 1889 exceptions (s 21(2)),
-- concurrent conditions and delivery rules (ss 27 to 29, 31 to 33), commercial units (s 35(7)), partial rejection (s 35A), the unpaid
-- seller's lien, stoppage in transit and re-sale (Part 5, which addresses the seller), specific performance (s 52, a judgment for the
-- court), interest (s 54), exclusion of implied terms (s 55, subject to the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, not deposited), conflict of
-- laws (s 56) and the Schedule's modified sections for contracts made before 19 May 1985 (asked as the contract date; such contracts
-- get an explicit "not covered" answer).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the individual to
-- whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass,
-- no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Sale of Goods Act 1979 -- buyers`

§§ `1(1), 2(1), 61(1), 62(4) -- does the Act reach my purchase`

-- s 1(1): the Act "applies to contracts of sale of goods made on or after (but not to those made before) 1 January 1894".
-- s 2(1): "A contract of sale of goods is a contract by which the seller transfers or agrees to transfer the property in goods to the
-- buyer for a money consideration, called the price." s 61(1): "goods" (a plain gloss: movable things) "includes all personal chattels
-- other than things in action and money". s 62(4): the Act "do[es] not apply to a transaction in the form of a contract of sale which is
-- intended to operate by way of mortgage, pledge, charge, or other security".
-- The Schedule modifies ss 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 35, 55 and 61 for contracts made before 19 May 1985 (paras 2 to 13); a purchase that old
-- gets an explicit "not covered" answer here rather than a guess at the modified text.
DECLARE `How the Act stands to my purchase` IS ONE OF
    `the Act applies`
    `the Act does not apply`
    `not covered by this encoding: a contract made before 19 May 1985 is read with the Schedule`

GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
      `I pay or agree to pay money for the goods` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the things are personal chattels, not things in action or money` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the sale is meant to operate as a mortgage, pledge, charge or other security` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `How the Act stands to my purchase`
`Does the Sale of Goods Act apply to my purchase` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF     NOT `I pay or agree to pay money for the goods`
            OR NOT `the things are personal chattels, not things in action or money`
            OR `the sale is meant to operate as a mortgage, pledge, charge or other security`
            OR DATE_SERIAL `the date the contract was made` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 1 1 1894)
        THEN `the Act does not apply`
        IF DATE_SERIAL `the date the contract was made` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 19 5 1985)
        THEN `not covered by this encoding: a contract made before 19 May 1985 is read with the Schedule`
        OTHERWISE `the Act applies`

§§ `3(2), 8 -- what price I must pay`

-- s 8(1): the price "may be fixed by the contract, or may be left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract, or may be determined
-- by the course of dealing". (2): otherwise "the buyer must pay a reasonable price". (3): what is reasonable "is a question of fact"
-- -- an input here, never guessed.
GIVEN `the price fixed by the contract, an agreed manner or our course of dealing` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `a reasonable price` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What price must I pay` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the price fixed by the contract, an agreed manner or our course of dealing`
    WHEN JUST p THEN p
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `a reasonable price`

-- s 3(1): capacity "is regulated by the general law". (2): "Where necessaries are sold and delivered to a minor or to a person who by
-- reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness is incompetent to contract, he must pay a reasonable price for them." (3): "necessaries"
-- (things I genuinely need) means goods "suitable to the condition in life of the minor or other person concerned and to his actual
-- requirements at the time of the sale and delivery". Outside (2) the answer is NOTHING: what an incapable buyer owes is the general
-- law's question, which the Act leaves alone.
GIVEN `I was a minor, or incompetent to contract through mental incapacity or drunkenness` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the goods suited my condition in life and my actual requirements then` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the goods were sold and delivered to me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a reasonable price` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What must I pay for goods bought while I could not contract` MEANS
    IF     `I was a minor, or incompetent to contract through mental incapacity or drunkenness`
       AND `the goods suited my condition in life and my actual requirements then`
       AND `the goods were sold and delivered to me`
    THEN JUST `a reasonable price`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `6, 7 -- goods that perish`

-- s 6: "Where there is a contract for the sale of specific goods, and the goods without the knowledge of the seller have perished at
-- the time when the contract is made, the contract is void."
-- s 7: "Where there is an agreement to sell specific goods and subsequently the goods, without any fault on the part of the seller or
-- buyer, perish before the risk passes to the buyer, the agreement is avoided."
-- s 61(1): "specific goods" (the very item agreed on) means "goods identified and agreed on at the time a contract of sale is made".
-- Neither section reaches a seller who knew, or a loss caused by someone's fault; then the Act does not end the contract this way.
DECLARE `What perishing does to my contract` IS ONE OF
    `void under section 6`
    `avoided under section 7`
    `neither section 6 nor section 7 ends it`

GIVEN `the goods are specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the goods had already perished when the contract was made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller knew they had perished` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the goods perished after an agreement to sell but before the risk passed to me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller or I was at fault` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What perishing does to my contract`
`What happens to my contract when the goods perish` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF     `the goods are specific goods`
           AND `the goods had already perished when the contract was made`
           AND NOT `the seller knew they had perished`
        THEN `void under section 6`
        IF     `the goods are specific goods`
           AND `the goods perished after an agreement to sell but before the risk passed to me`
           AND NOT `the seller or I was at fault`
        THEN `avoided under section 7`
        OTHERWISE `neither section 6 nor section 7 ends it`

§§ `12 -- what the seller promises about title`

-- s 12(1): "there is an implied condition on the part of the seller that in the case of a sale he has a right to sell the goods"; (2)
-- warranties that the goods are free "from any charge or encumbrance not disclosed or known to the buyer" and that "the buyer will enjoy
-- quiet possession". (3): the contract where it appears "that the seller should transfer only such title as he or a third person may
-- have". (4), (5): then only warranties that known charges "have been disclosed" and that the seller, the third person and those
-- claiming through them will not disturb "the buyer's quiet possession".
DECLARE `The seller's promise about title` IS ONE OF
    `a condition that the seller has the right to sell, with warranties of no undisclosed charge and of quiet possession`
    `only warranties that known charges were disclosed and that my quiet possession will not be disturbed`

GIVEN `the contract shows the seller is to transfer only such title as the seller or a third person has` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The seller's promise about title`
`What does the seller promise me about title` MEANS
    IF `the contract shows the seller is to transfer only such title as the seller or a third person has`
    THEN `only warranties that known charges were disclosed and that my quiet possession will not be disturbed`
    ELSE `a condition that the seller has the right to sell, with warranties of no undisclosed charge and of quiet possession`

§§ `13, 14, 15 -- the implied conditions about the goods`

-- s 13(1): in a sale "by description" there is "an implied condition that the goods will correspond with the description"; (3) goods
-- "selected by the buyer" from a display can still be sold by description.
-- s 14(1): apart from s 14 and s 15 "there is no implied condition or warranty about the quality or fitness" of goods.
-- s 14(2): "Where the seller sells goods in the course of a business, there is an implied condition that the goods supplied under the
-- contract are of satisfactory quality." (2A): satisfactory means "the standard that a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory,
-- taking account of any description of the goods, the price (if relevant) and all the other relevant circumstances" -- asked as a fact.
-- s 14(2C): the condition "does not extend to any matter making the quality of goods unsatisfactory -- (a) which is specifically drawn
-- to the buyer's attention before the contract is made; (b) where the buyer examines the goods before the contract is made, which that
-- examination ought to reveal; or (c) in the case of a contract for sale by sample, which would have been apparent on a reasonable
-- examination of the sample."
-- s 14(3): where the buyer "makes known ... any particular purpose", the goods must be "reasonably fit for that purpose", "except where
-- the circumstances show that the buyer does not rely, or that it is unreasonable for him to rely, on the skill or judgment of the
-- seller". (The credit-broker limb, (3)(b), is not encoded.)
-- s 14(5): these apply to "a person who in the course of a business is acting as agent for another", "except where that other is not
-- selling in the course of a business and either the buyer knows that fact or reasonable steps are taken to bring it to the notice of
-- the buyer before the contract is made". s 61(1): "business" "includes a profession and the activities of any Government department
-- or local or public authority".
-- s 15(2): in a sale "by sample" there are implied conditions "(a) that the bulk will correspond with the sample in quality; (b) that the
-- goods will be free from any defect, making their quality unsatisfactory, which would not be apparent on reasonable examination of the
-- sample". s 15(2)(b) is not limited to business sellers.
DECLARE `My purchase` HAS
    `the date the contract was made`                                             IS A DATE
    `the seller sells in the course of a business`                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `a business agent sells for an owner who is not selling in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I knew, or reasonable steps were taken to tell me, that the owner was not in business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods were sold by description`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods do not correspond with the description`                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods are not of a quality a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the defect was specifically drawn to my attention before the contract`      IS A BOOLEAN
    `I examined the goods before the contract and that examination ought to have revealed the defect` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I told the seller a particular purpose for the goods`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods are not reasonably fit for that purpose`                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `I did not rely, or it was unreasonable to rely, on the seller's skill or judgment` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is a sale by sample`                                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the bulk does not correspond with the sample in quality`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `the defect would have been apparent on a reasonable examination of the sample` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have accepted the goods`                                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contract is severable`                                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contract lets me reject even after acceptance`                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller has shown that I do not deal as consumer`                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller has shown the breach is so slight that rejecting would be unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contract shows an intention that section 15A should not apply`            IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the seller treated as selling in the course of a business` IF
        p's `the seller sells in the course of a business`
    OR  (    p's `a business agent sells for an owner who is not selling in the course of a business`
         AND NOT p's `I knew, or reasonable steps were taken to tell me, that the owner was not in business`)

GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the goods break the implied condition of correspondence with description` IF
    p's `the goods were sold by description` AND p's `the goods do not correspond with the description`

GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the goods break the implied condition of satisfactory quality` IF
        `Is the seller treated as selling in the course of a business` p
    AND p's `the goods are not of a quality a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory`
    AND NOT p's `the defect was specifically drawn to my attention before the contract`
    AND NOT p's `I examined the goods before the contract and that examination ought to have revealed the defect`
    AND NOT (p's `it is a sale by sample` AND p's `the defect would have been apparent on a reasonable examination of the sample`)

GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the goods break the implied condition of fitness for my purpose` IF
        `Is the seller treated as selling in the course of a business` p
    AND p's `I told the seller a particular purpose for the goods`
    AND p's `the goods are not reasonably fit for that purpose`
    AND NOT p's `I did not rely, or it was unreasonable to rely, on the seller's skill or judgment`

-- s 15(2)(b) is read with the same "defect" as the quality facts: a defect making the quality unsatisfactory that a reasonable
-- examination of the sample would not show.
GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the goods break an implied condition of a sale by sample` IF
        p's `it is a sale by sample`
    AND (    p's `the bulk does not correspond with the sample in quality`
         OR  (    p's `the goods are not of a quality a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory`
              AND NOT p's `the defect would have been apparent on a reasonable examination of the sample`))

GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken a condition implied by sections 13 to 15` IF
        `Do the goods break the implied condition of correspondence with description` p
    OR  `Do the goods break the implied condition of satisfactory quality` p
    OR  `Do the goods break the implied condition of fitness for my purpose` p
    OR  `Do the goods break an implied condition of a sale by sample` p

§§ `11(3), 15A, 53(1) -- may I reject, or only claim damages`

-- s 11(3): "Subject to section 35A, where a contract of sale is not severable and the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them, the
-- breach of a condition to be fulfilled by the seller can only be treated as a breach of warranty, and not as a ground for rejecting the
-- goods and treating the contract as repudiated, unless there is an express or implied term of the contract to that effect."
-- s 15A(1): where the buyer would have the right to reject for breach of a condition implied by s 13, 14 or 15 "but (b) the breach is so
-- slight that it would be unreasonable for the buyer to reject them, then, if the buyer does not deal as consumer, the breach is not to
-- be treated as a breach of condition but may be treated as a breach of warranty". (2): "unless a contrary intention appears". (3): "It
-- is for the seller to show" the slightness. s 61(4B): "it is for a seller claiming that the buyer does not deal as consumer to show
-- that he does not." "Deals as consumer" is borrowed from Part 1 of the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (s 61(4A)): asked as a fact.
-- s 53(1): for a breach of warranty the buyer "is not ... entitled to reject the goods; but he may (a) set up against the seller the
-- breach of warranty in diminution or extinction of the price; or (b) maintain an action against the seller for damages".
-- s 61(1): "warranty" (a lesser term) gives "a claim for damages, but not ... a right to reject the goods and treat the contract as
-- repudiated". A severable contract the buyer has partly accepted turns on s 31(2) ("a question in each case"): not covered.
DECLARE `My remedy for the goods` IS ONE OF
    `I may reject the goods and treat the contract as repudiated`
    `I may claim damages or set the breach against the price, but not reject`
    `the implied conditions give me no remedy`
    `not covered by this encoding: a severable contract partly accepted turns on section 31(2)`
    `not covered by this encoding: a contract made before 19 May 1985 is read with the Schedule`

GIVEN p IS A `My purchase`
GIVETH A `My remedy for the goods`
`What can I do about the goods` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF DATE_SERIAL (p's `the date the contract was made`) LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL (DATE_FROM_DMY 19 5 1985)
        THEN `not covered by this encoding: a contract made before 19 May 1985 is read with the Schedule`
        IF NOT `Has the seller broken a condition implied by sections 13 to 15` p
        THEN `the implied conditions give me no remedy`
        IF p's `I have accepted the goods` AND p's `the contract is severable`
        THEN `not covered by this encoding: a severable contract partly accepted turns on section 31(2)`
        IF p's `I have accepted the goods` AND NOT p's `the contract lets me reject even after acceptance`
        THEN `I may claim damages or set the breach against the price, but not reject`
        IF     p's `the seller has shown that I do not deal as consumer`
           AND p's `the seller has shown the breach is so slight that rejecting would be unreasonable`
           AND NOT p's `the contract shows an intention that section 15A should not apply`
        THEN `I may claim damages or set the breach against the price, but not reject`
        OTHERWISE `I may reject the goods and treat the contract as repudiated`

§§ `35 -- have I accepted the goods`

-- s 35(1): the buyer is deemed to have accepted "(a) when he intimates to the seller that he has accepted them; or (b) when the goods
-- have been delivered to him and he does any act in relation to them which is inconsistent with the ownership of the seller". (2): a
-- buyer who "has not previously examined them ... is not deemed to have accepted them under subsection (1) until he has had a reasonable
-- opportunity of examining them". (3): "Where the buyer deals as consumer, the buyer cannot lose his right to rely on subsection (2) by
-- agreement, waiver or otherwise." (4): also "when after the lapse of a reasonable time he retains the goods without intimating to the
-- seller that he has rejected them" (s 59: "a question of fact", an input). (6): not "merely because -- (a) he asks for, or agrees to,
-- their repair by or under an arrangement with the seller; or (b) the goods are delivered to another under a sub-sale". The repair and
-- sub-sale facts are deliberately inert: s 35(6) makes neither a ground of acceptance on its own.
DECLARE `What I did with the goods` HAS
    `the goods have been delivered to me`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `I told the seller I accepted them`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `I did an act inconsistent with the seller's ownership`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `I had examined them or had a reasonable opportunity to examine them`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `I agreed to give up my chance to examine them`                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller has shown that I do not deal as consumer`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `I kept them beyond a reasonable time without telling the seller I rejected them` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I asked the seller to repair them`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `I passed them on under a sub-sale`                                           IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN d IS A `What I did with the goods`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I accepted the goods` IF
        (    (   d's `I told the seller I accepted them`
              OR (d's `the goods have been delivered to me` AND d's `I did an act inconsistent with the seller's ownership`))
         AND (   d's `I had examined them or had a reasonable opportunity to examine them`
              OR (d's `I agreed to give up my chance to examine them` AND d's `the seller has shown that I do not deal as consumer`)))
    OR  (d's `the goods have been delivered to me` AND d's `I kept them beyond a reasonable time without telling the seller I rejected them`)

§§ `34, 36 -- examining the goods, and rejected goods`

-- s 34: "Unless otherwise agreed, when the seller tenders delivery of the goods to the buyer, he is bound on request to afford the buyer a
-- reasonable opportunity of examining the goods".
GIVEN `the seller has tendered delivery` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I asked to examine the goods` IS A BOOLEAN
      `we agreed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the seller let me examine the goods` IF
    `the seller has tendered delivery` AND `I asked to examine the goods` AND NOT `we agreed otherwise`

-- s 36: "Unless otherwise agreed, where goods are delivered to the buyer, and he refuses to accept them, having the right to do so, he
-- is not bound to return them to the seller, but it is sufficient if he intimates to the seller that he refuses to accept them."
GIVEN `I agreed to return rejected goods` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I return goods I have rightly rejected` IF `I agreed to return rejected goods`

§§ `17, 18, 20 -- when the goods become mine, and at my risk`

-- s 17(1): property in specific or ascertained goods passes "at such time as the parties to the contract intend". s 18: "Unless a
-- different intention appears", the Rules apply. Rule 1: an "unconditional contract for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable
-- state" -- property passes "when the contract is made", even if payment or delivery is postponed. Rule 2: where the seller "is bound to
-- do something to the goods for the purpose of putting them into a deliverable state", not "until the thing is done and the buyer has
-- notice that it has been done". Rule 3: the same where the seller must "weigh, measure, test, or do some other act" to fix the price.
-- Rule 4: goods "on approval or on sale or return" pass "(i) when he signifies his approval or acceptance to the seller or does any
-- other act adopting the transaction; (ii) if he ... retains the goods without giving notice of rejection", on expiry of the time fixed
-- for return or, if none, "a reasonable time". Rule 5(1): unascertained or future goods by description pass when goods "in a
-- deliverable state are unconditionally appropriated to the contract" by one party "with the assent of" the other. s 16: no property in
-- unascertained goods "unless and until the goods are ascertained". "Property" (s 61(1)) is ownership, "the general property in goods".
-- Where a different intention appears, s 17's question of what the parties intended is not answered here.
DECLARE `How I bought the goods` IS ONE OF
    `specific goods in a deliverable state, unconditionally`
    `specific goods the seller must still put into a deliverable state`
    `specific goods the seller must still weigh, measure or test to fix the price`
    `goods on approval or on sale or return`
    `unascertained or future goods by description`

DECLARE `The steps taken` HAS
    `a different intention appears`                                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller has done the thing and I have notice of it`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `I signified approval or did an act adopting the transaction`                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `I kept the goods without notice of rejection past the fixed or a reasonable time` IS A BOOLEAN
    `goods in a deliverable state were unconditionally appropriated with assent`  IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Whether the goods are mine` IS ONE OF
    `the property has passed to me`
    `the property has not passed to me`
    `not covered by this encoding: the parties' own intention governs under section 17`

GIVEN how IS A `How I bought the goods`, s IS A `The steps taken`
GIVETH A `Whether the goods are mine`
`Have the goods become mine` MEANS
    IF s's `a different intention appears`
    THEN `not covered by this encoding: the parties' own intention governs under section 17`
    ELSE IF `the rule is met`
         THEN `the property has passed to me`
         ELSE `the property has not passed to me`
  WHERE
    `the rule is met` MEANS
        CONSIDER how
        WHEN `specific goods in a deliverable state, unconditionally` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `specific goods the seller must still put into a deliverable state` THEN s's `the seller has done the thing and I have notice of it`
        WHEN `specific goods the seller must still weigh, measure or test to fix the price` THEN s's `the seller has done the thing and I have notice of it`
        WHEN `goods on approval or on sale or return` THEN
                s's `I signified approval or did an act adopting the transaction`
             OR s's `I kept the goods without notice of rejection past the fixed or a reasonable time`
        WHEN `unascertained or future goods by description` THEN s's `goods in a deliverable state were unconditionally appropriated with assent`

-- s 20(1): "Unless otherwise agreed, the goods remain at the seller's risk until the property in them is transferred to the buyer, but
-- when the property in them is transferred to the buyer the goods are at the buyer's risk whether delivery has been made or not." (2):
-- where delivery "has been delayed through the fault of either buyer or seller, the goods are at the risk of the party at fault as
-- regards any loss which might not have occurred but for such fault". s 61(1): "fault" means "wrongful act or default".
-- "Risk" (a plain gloss: who bears an accidental loss). Agreement otherwise is an input that ends the inquiry (NOTHING).
GIVEN `we agreed otherwise about risk` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the property has passed to me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the loss would not have happened but for delivery delayed by my fault` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the loss would not have happened but for delivery delayed by the seller's fault` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I bear the loss of the goods` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `we agreed otherwise about risk` THEN NOTHING
        IF `the loss would not have happened but for delivery delayed by my fault` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the loss would not have happened but for delivery delayed by the seller's fault` THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST `the property has passed to me`

§§ `21, 23, 24, 25 -- do I own goods bought from someone who did not`

-- s 21(1): where goods are sold by a non-owner without the owner's authority or consent, "the buyer acquires no better title to the
-- goods than the seller had, unless the owner of the goods is by his conduct precluded from denying the seller's authority to sell".
-- s 23: where the seller "has a voidable title to them, but his title has not been avoided at the time of the sale, the buyer acquires a
-- good title to the goods, provided he buys them in good faith and without notice of the seller's defect of title".
-- s 24: where a seller "continues or is in possession of the goods" after selling them, delivery to a person "receiving the same in good
-- faith and without notice of the previous sale, has the same effect as if the person making the delivery or transfer were expressly
-- authorised by the owner".
-- s 25: delivery by a buyer in possession with the seller's consent, to one receiving in good faith without notice of the original
-- seller's rights, "has the same effect as if the person making the delivery or transfer were a mercantile agent in possession ...
-- with the consent of the owner" -- an effect the Factors Act 1889 supplies (s 21(2)(a)), not deposited: not covered.
-- s 61(2): good faith means "in fact done honestly, whether it is done negligently or not". Whether a seller's title was void or
-- voidable is general law (s 62(2)): asked as a fact.
DECLARE `How I came by the goods` HAS
    `the seller owned the goods or sold with the owner's authority or consent`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the owner's conduct precludes denying the seller's authority`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller had a voidable title not yet avoided when I bought`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller had already sold them to someone else but stayed in possession`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the seller was a buyer in possession with the original seller's consent`      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the goods were delivered or transferred to me`                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `I acted honestly and without notice of the defect, earlier sale or other right` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `My title` IS ONE OF
    `I have a good title`
    `I have no better title than the seller had`
    `not covered by this encoding: section 25 takes its effect from the Factors Act 1889`

GIVEN c IS A `How I came by the goods`
GIVETH A `My title`
`Do I own the goods I bought` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF    c's `the seller owned the goods or sold with the owner's authority or consent`
           OR c's `the owner's conduct precludes denying the seller's authority`
        THEN `I have a good title`
        IF c's `the seller had a voidable title not yet avoided when I bought`
           AND c's `I acted honestly and without notice of the defect, earlier sale or other right`
        THEN `I have a good title`
        IF     c's `the seller had already sold them to someone else but stayed in possession`
           AND c's `the goods were delivered or transferred to me`
           AND c's `I acted honestly and without notice of the defect, earlier sale or other right`
        THEN `I have a good title`
        IF     c's `the seller was a buyer in possession with the original seller's consent`
           AND c's `the goods were delivered or transferred to me`
           AND c's `I acted honestly and without notice of the defect, earlier sale or other right`
        THEN `not covered by this encoding: section 25 takes its effect from the Factors Act 1889`
        OTHERWISE `I have no better title than the seller had`

§§ `30 -- the wrong quantity`

-- s 30(1): if the seller delivers less, "the buyer may reject them, but if the buyer accepts the goods so delivered, he must pay for them
-- at the contract rate". (2): if more, "the buyer may accept the goods included in the contract and reject the rest, or he may reject
-- the whole". (2A) [4/2014]: "A buyer who does not deal as consumer may not" reject a short delivery under (1), or reject the whole of
-- an excess under (2), "if the shortfall or, as the case may be, excess is so slight that it would be unreasonable". (2B): "It is for the
-- seller to show" that. (3): accepting the whole of a larger delivery, "he must pay for them at the contract rate". (4): subject to
-- "usage of trade, special agreement, or course of dealing" -- not modelled.
GIVEN `units delivered` IS A NUMBER
      `units contracted` IS A NUMBER
      `the seller has shown that I do not deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller has shown the difference is so slight that rejecting would be unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I reject the whole delivery for the wrong quantity` IF
        NOT (`units delivered` EQUALS `units contracted`)
    AND NOT (    `the seller has shown that I do not deal as consumer`
             AND `the seller has shown the difference is so slight that rejecting would be unreasonable`)

GIVEN `units delivered` IS A NUMBER
      `units contracted` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I keep the contract quantity and reject the rest` IF
    `units delivered` GREATER THAN `units contracted`

GIVEN `units I keep` IS A NUMBER
      `the contract rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What must I pay for the units I keep` MEANS `units I keep` TIMES `the contract rate`

§§ `37, 49, 50 -- what the seller can claim from me`

-- s 49(1): where "the property in the goods has passed to the buyer and he wrongfully neglects or refuses to pay for the goods", the seller
-- "may maintain an action against him for the price". (2): also where "the price is payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery and
-- the buyer wrongfully neglects or refuses to pay", "although the property in the goods has not passed".
GIVEN `the property has passed to me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the price is payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I have wrongfully not paid` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the seller sue me for the price` IF
        `I have wrongfully not paid`
    AND (`the property has passed to me` OR `the price is payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery`)

-- s 50(1), (2): for wrongfully neglecting or refusing "to accept and pay for the goods", damages are "the estimated loss directly and
-- naturally resulting". (3): with an available market, prima facie "the difference between the contract price and the market or current
-- price". The text does not say which way round; the seller's loss is read as contract price less market price, never below zero -- an
-- INFERENCE from "loss" in (2).
GIVEN `the contract price` IS A NUMBER
      `the market price when I should have accepted` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What can the seller claim from me for not accepting the goods` MEANS
    IF `the contract price` GREATER THAN `the market price when I should have accepted`
    THEN `the contract price` MINUS `the market price when I should have accepted`
    ELSE 0

-- s 37(1): when the seller "is ready and willing to deliver the goods, and requests the buyer to take delivery, and the buyer does not
-- within a reasonable time after such request take delivery", the buyer "is liable to the seller for any loss occasioned by his neglect
-- or refusal to take delivery, and also for a reasonable charge for the care and custody of the goods". The loss and the charge are
-- inputs (s 59: a reasonable time is a question of fact).
GIVEN `the seller was ready and willing and asked me to take delivery` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I did not take delivery within a reasonable time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the seller's loss from my not taking delivery` IS A NUMBER
      `a reasonable charge for care and custody` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What can the seller claim from me for not taking delivery` MEANS
    IF `the seller was ready and willing and asked me to take delivery` AND `I did not take delivery within a reasonable time`
    THEN `the seller's loss from my not taking delivery` PLUS `a reasonable charge for care and custody`
    ELSE 0

§§ `51, 53 -- my damages`

-- s 51(1), (2): where the seller "wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver", the buyer may sue for "the estimated loss directly and
-- naturally resulting". (3): with an available market, prima facie "the difference between the contract price and the market or current
-- price". The direction and the floor at zero are an INFERENCE from "loss" in (2), as for s 50.
GIVEN `the contract price` IS A NUMBER
      `the market price when the goods should have been delivered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What can I claim for non-delivery` MEANS
    IF `the market price when the goods should have been delivered` GREATER THAN `the contract price`
    THEN `the market price when the goods should have been delivered` MINUS `the contract price`
    ELSE 0

-- s 53(3): "In the case of breach of warranty of quality, such loss is prima facie the difference between the value of the goods at the
-- time of delivery to the buyer and the value they would have had if they had fulfilled the warranty."
GIVEN `the value they would have had as warranted` IS A NUMBER
      `the value at delivery` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is my loss for goods below the warranted quality` MEANS
    IF `the value they would have had as warranted` GREATER THAN `the value at delivery`
    THEN `the value they would have had as warranted` MINUS `the value at delivery`
    ELSE 0

-- s 53(1)(a): the buyer may "set up against the seller the breach of warranty in diminution or extinction of the price"; (4): doing so
-- "does not prevent him from maintaining an action for the same breach of warranty if he has suffered further damage". The set-off
-- extinguishes the unpaid price at most: what is left is never below zero (from "extinction").
GIVEN `the price I still owe` IS A NUMBER
      `my loss from the breach of warranty` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of the price must I still pay after setting off the breach` MEANS
    IF `my loss from the breach of warranty` GREATER THAN `the price I still owe`
    THEN 0
    ELSE `the price I still owe` MINUS `my loss from the breach of warranty`

§§ `57 -- auctions`

-- s 57(2): a sale by auction "is complete when the auctioneer announces its completion by the fall of the hammer, or in other customary
-- manner; and until the announcement is made any bidder may retract his bid". (4): where the sale "is not notified to be subject to a
-- right to bid by or on behalf of the seller, it is not lawful for the seller to bid himself or to employ any person to bid at the
-- sale". (5): "A sale contravening subsection (4) may be treated as fraudulent by the buyer." (6): where a right to bid "is expressly
-- reserved (but not otherwise) the seller or any one person on his behalf may bid".
GIVEN `the auctioneer has announced the sale complete` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I retract my bid` IF NOT `the auctioneer has announced the sale complete`

GIVEN `the seller or someone employed by the seller bid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the sale was notified to be subject to a right to bid for the seller` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I treat the auction sale as fraudulent` IF
    `the seller or someone employed by the seller bid` AND NOT `the sale was notified to be subject to a right to bid for the seller`
